# Colouring the book's code

A source page shows the code each section of Loom's own book writes, and code reads more easily in colour. A reader who sees the keywords, the strings and the comments apart finds the shape of a function before reading a word of it.

The site's build colours the code, and neither the server nor the browser ever runs a highlighter. The colours come from Shiki, a highlighter that reads code with the same TextMate grammars VS Code uses. Its grammars and the engine that runs them weigh a few megabytes, far more than a reader should download to see colour. So the build runs Shiki over the whole book once, right after it weaves the book, and writes each run of code out with its colours already worked out, as marks. The server reads the book in colour and hands each source page its chapter, marks and all, inside the model. The browser draws the marks the way it draws the links.

Colouring belongs to the build rather than to the server because of what Shiki costs while it works. Colouring the whole book takes about four seconds and churns through hundreds of megabytes of short-lived memory. The engine's own memory grows past a hundred megabytes as it goes, and never shrinks. A server that coloured each chapter on its first visit grew to about 800 megabytes once a reader had opened every chapter. A server that reads the book already coloured stays near 320.

## Which grammar reads which language

The weave names the language of each run of code as the loom wrote it, in a chapter's frontmatter or a section's specifier: `typescript`, `sh` or `rs`. Shiki names its grammars its own way, so `GRAMMARS` maps each language the book writes in to the grammar that reads it. A language with no grammar here, such as a `.gitignore` file's, draws in plain ink.

=>

const GRAMMARS: ReadonlyMap<string, BundledLanguage> = new Map([
  ['typescript', 'typescript'],
  ['javascript', 'javascript'],
  ['json', 'json'],
  ['css', 'css'],
  ['html', 'html'],
  ['markdown', 'markdown'],
  ['yaml', 'yaml'],
  ['toml', 'toml'],
  ['dockerfile', 'dockerfile'],
  ['sh', 'shellscript'],
  ['c', 'c'],
  ['lua', 'lua'],
  ['rs', 'rust'],
  ['scm', 'scheme'],
])

const grammarOf = (language: string): Option.Option<BundledLanguage> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(GRAMMARS.get(language))

## A theme that names kinds

A grammar gives each token a scope, a dotted name such as `keyword.control.import` or `string.quoted.single`. A Shiki theme maps scopes to colours. A rule in it matches a scope and every scope that extends it, so a rule for `string` matches `string.quoted.single` too. Where two rules match one token, the more specific rule wins.

The site's theme maps scopes to the six kinds of token rather than to colours. So where Shiki would answer with a colour it answers with a kind, and the colours stay in the stylesheet beside the rest of the paper scheme. A token that no rule names, such as a variable, stays plain.

A few rules hand a scope back to plain, because a broader rule would colour it wrongly. An operator such as `+` sits under `keyword.operator`, which would make it a keyword, while the words `typeof` and `new` that sit under the same scope are keywords. The code inside a template string's `${…}` sits under `string`, but reads as code.

=>

const PLAIN = 'plain'

const SCOPES: ReadonlyArray<readonly [TokenKind | typeof PLAIN, ReadonlyArray<string>]> = [
  ['comment', ['comment', 'punctuation.definition.comment']],
  ['string', ['string', 'punctuation.definition.string']],
  ['constant', ['constant', 'support.constant', 'keyword.other.unit']],
  [
    'keyword',
    [
      'keyword',
      'storage',
      'keyword.operator.expression',
      'keyword.operator.new',
      'keyword.operator.word',
      'punctuation.definition.template-expression',
    ],
  ],
  ['function', ['entity.name.function', 'support.function', 'entity.other.attribute-name']],
  [
    'type',
    [
      'entity.name.type',
      'entity.name.class',
      'entity.name.tag',
      'entity.other.inherited-class',
      'support.type',
      'support.class',
      'storage.type.primitive',
    ],
  ],
  [PLAIN, ['keyword.operator', 'punctuation', 'meta.template.expression', 'meta.embedded']],
]

const THEME: ThemeRegistration = {
  name: 'loom-kinds',
  type: 'light',
  fg: PLAIN,
  bg: PLAIN,
  settings: [
    { settings: { foreground: PLAIN } },
    ...Array.map(SCOPES, ([kind, scope]) => ({ scope: [...scope], settings: { foreground: kind } })),
  ],
}

## Loading the highlighter

`makeHighlighter` loads Shiki once, with the theme and the grammars the book needs. It runs the grammars on Oniguruma, the regular-expression engine TextMate grammars are written for, compiled to WebAssembly. Shiki also offers an engine written in JavaScript, which needs no WebAssembly, but on the book it runs five times slower.

=>

const makeHighlighter: Effect.Effect<HighlighterCore> = Effect.promise(() =>
  createHighlighterCore({
    themes: [THEME],
    langs: Array.map(Array.dedupe(GRAMMARS.values()), (grammar) => bundledLanguages[grammar]),
    engine: createOnigurumaEngine(import('shiki/wasm')),
  }),
)

## Anchors stay out of the grammar

A `::[…]` anchor belongs to Loom, not to the language around it, so a grammar reads one as broken code. A grammar that has lost its place can colour the rest of the run wrongly. So `blanked` writes underscores over each anchor before Shiki reads the code. Nearly every language reads a run of underscores as a name. The code also keeps its length, so every mark lands where it belongs in the code as the loom wrote it.

=>

const blanked = (block: CodeBlock): string =>
  Array.reduce(
    block.links,
    block.code,
    (code, link) =>
      code.slice(0, link.offset) + '_'.repeat(link.length) + code.slice(link.offset + link.length),
  )

## Tokens become marks

`marksOf` reads a run of code into its marks. Shiki cuts each line into tokens, and each token carries its text, where it starts in the code, and what the theme gives it, which here is a kind or plain. A plain token draws in ink, so it needs no mark. A mark that falls on an anchor's underscores is dropped too, because the anchor draws as a link.

=>

const kindOf = S.decodeUnknownOption(TokenKind)

const overlaps = (mark: CodeMark, link: CodeLink): boolean =>
  mark.offset < link.offset + link.length && link.offset < mark.offset + mark.length

const marksOf = (highlighter: HighlighterCore, block: CodeBlock): ReadonlyArray<CodeMark> =>
  Option.match(grammarOf(block.language), {
    onNone: () => [],
    onSome: (lang) =>
      pipe(
        highlighter.codeToTokensBase(blanked(block), { lang, theme: THEME.name }),
        Array.flatten,
        Array.map((token) =>
          Option.map(
            kindOf(token.color),
            (kind): CodeMark => ({ offset: token.offset, length: token.content.length, kind }),
          ),
        ),
        Array.getSomes,
        Array.filter((mark) => !Array.some(block.links, (link) => overlaps(mark, link))),
      ),
  })

## The whole book in colour

`colouredCorpus` colours every run of code in a corpus and leaves its other blocks as they are. The build hands it the whole book.

=>

const colouredBlock =
  (highlighter: HighlighterCore) =>
  (block: Block): Block =>
    block.type === 'code' ? { ...block, marks: marksOf(highlighter, block) } : block

const colouredCorpus = (highlighter: HighlighterCore, corpus: Corpus): Corpus => ({
  ...corpus,
  pages: Array.map(corpus.pages, (page) => ({
    ...page,
    blocks: Array.map(page.blocks, colouredBlock(highlighter)),
  })),
})

## Once, as the site is built

The colouring runs as a program of its own, which the site's build runs right after it weaves the book. The program reads the woven book from `source.woven.json`, colours it, and writes the result to `source.coloured.json`, the file the server reads.

The model's `Corpus` schema reads and writes both files, through `S.fromJsonString`, which joins parsing JSON to the schema. Decoding the woven book drops what the site never draws, such as where each block came from in its loom. Encoding the coloured book writes each mark as the triple the model sends to the browser. A woven book that is missing or does not decode means the build is broken, so the program fails, and the build stops with it.

=>

const here = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))

const BookFile = S.fromJsonString(Corpus)

const colourBook = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const fs = yield* FileSystem.FileSystem
  const book = yield* fs
    .readFileString(join(here, 'source.woven.json'))
    .pipe(Effect.flatMap(S.decodeUnknownEffect(BookFile)))
  const highlighter = yield* makeHighlighter
  const coloured = yield* S.encodeEffect(BookFile)(colouredCorpus(highlighter, book))
  yield* fs.writeFileString(join(here, 'source.coloured.json'), coloured)
})

colourBook.pipe(Effect.provide(BunServices.layer), BunRuntime.runMain)

## The program {Tangle}[colour.ts]

The program draws on `effect` for its combinators, the file system and the schemas, on Bun's platform for its file services and the runtime edge it starts from, on Shiki's core, its WebAssembly engine and its grammars, and on the model for the shapes it reads and writes.

=>

import { Array, Effect, FileSystem, Option, pipe, Schema as S } from 'effect'
import { BunRuntime, BunServices } from '@effect/platform-bun'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'
import { createHighlighterCore, type HighlighterCore, type ThemeRegistration } from 'shiki/core'
import { createOnigurumaEngine } from 'shiki/engine/oniguruma'
import { bundledLanguages, type BundledLanguage } from 'shiki/langs'
import {
  Corpus,
  TokenKind,
  type Block,
  type CodeBlock,
  type CodeLink,
  type CodeMark,
} from './model'

